      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A post office opened in Volente in 1886, but closed in 1909 and mail was sent to Cedar Park.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/volente-tx",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The 1930s saw part of Volente submerged by Lake Travis, forcing residents to move to higher ground.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/volente-tx",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During the 1940s and early 1950s the community was briefly called Dodd City after a local realtor, before reverting to Volente.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/volente-tx",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1990 Volente housed a church, a combined fire hall‑community center, several businesses, and the Anderson Mill Museum.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
